From: microvm <microvm@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] x86/mm: optionally keep the kernel image's alignment gaps

mark_rodata_ro() frees the 2 MiB alignment padding between text and rodata
and between rodata and data. Freeing it makes it writable again, page by
page, in both the kernel mapping and the direct map, after splitting their
large pages: in a 64 MiB microVM that is ~1M cycles, ~14% of the boot.

"keep_image_gaps" skips that. The padding stays reserved, read-only and
non-executable, so no protection changes. The cost is ~2.5 MiB that never
becomes free memory: to a VMM that backs guest RAM on demand, untouched
padding costs nothing.

--- a/arch/x86/mm/init_64.c	2026-10-30 13:06:41.000000000 +0100
+++ b/arch/x86/mm/init_64.c	2026-11-04 08:49:55.808261465 +0100
@@ -1408,6 +1408,23 @@
 
 int kernel_set_to_readonly;
 
+/*
+ * "keep_image_gaps": don't free the 2 MiB alignment padding after the
+ * kernel text and after rodata. Freeing it means making those pages
+ * writable again one 4 KiB page at a time, in both the kernel mapping and
+ * the direct map: most of the CPA work of a small guest's boot. The cost
+ * is the padding (~2.5 MiB) not becoming free memory. It stays reserved,
+ * read-only and non-executable.
+ */
+static bool keep_image_gaps __ro_after_init;
+
+static int __init parse_keep_image_gaps(char *arg)
+{
+	keep_image_gaps = true;
+	return 0;
+}
+early_param("keep_image_gaps", parse_keep_image_gaps);
+
 void mark_rodata_ro(void)
 {
 	unsigned long start = PFN_ALIGN(_text);
@@ -1448,6 +1465,8 @@
 	set_memory_ro(start, (end-start) >> PAGE_SHIFT);
 #endif
 
+	if (keep_image_gaps)
+		return;
 	free_kernel_image_pages("unused kernel image (text/rodata gap)",
 				(void *)text_end, (void *)rodata_start);
 	free_kernel_image_pages("unused kernel image (rodata/data gap)",
